<p><a name="footnote2"></a><sup>2</sup> Alan  Greenspan, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/boarddocs/speeches/2003/20030829/default.htm">Monetary  Policy under Uncertainty</a>,&rdquo; at a symposium sponsored by the Federal Reserve  Bank of Kansas City, Jackson Hole, Wyoming, August 29, 2003.</p>
<p><a name="footnote3"></a><sup>3</sup> Taylor, John B., 1993. &ldquo;<a href="https://web.stanford.edu/~johntayl/Onlinepaperscombinedbyyear/1993/Discretion_versus_Policy_Rules_in_Practice.pdf">Discretion  Versus Policy Rules in Practice</a>,&rdquo;<em> Carnegie-Rochester Series on Public  Policy</em>. 39: 195-214.</p>
<p><a name="footnote4"></a><sup>4</sup> McCallum,  B. T., 1988. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0167223188900115">Robustness  Properties of a Rule for Monetary Policy</a>,&rdquo; <em>Carnegie Rochester Conference  Series on Public Policy</em>, 29: 173–204, and Taylor, John B., 1999. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.nber.org/system/files/chapters/c7412/c7412.pdf">Introduction</a> to <em>Monetary Policy Rules</em>,&rdquo; ed. John B. Taylor, University of Chicago  Press, 1–14.</p>
<p><a name="footnote5"></a><sup>5</sup> This analogy was suggested by Patrick Minford and referenced in: Levin, Andrew  T. and John C. Williams, 2003. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030439320300059X">Robust  Monetary Policy with Competing Reference Models</a>,&rdquo; <em>Journal of Monetary  Economics</em>, July, 50: 945–75. </p>
<p><a name="footnote6"></a><sup>6</sup> John C.  Williams, &ldquo;<a href="/newsevents/speeches/2024/wil240503">Connecting  Theory and Practice</a>,&rdquo; remarks at the Hoover Institution Policy  Conference, Stanford, California, May 3, 2024.</p>
<p><a name="footnote7"></a><sup>7</sup> Board  of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20120125c.htm">Federal  Reserve issues FOMC statement of longer-run goals and policy strategy</a>,  January 25, 2012.</p>
<p><a name="footnote8"></a><sup>8</sup> Alternative  characterizations of robust policy include robust control and robust optimal  policy; for early references, see Levin, Andrew T. and John C. Williams, 2003.  &ldquo;<a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030439320300059X">Robust  Monetary Policy with Competing Reference Models</a>,&rdquo; <em>Journal of Monetary  Economics</em>, July, 50: 945–75.</p>
<p><a name="footnote9"></a><sup>9</sup> Levin,  Andrew T. and John C. Williams, 2003. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030439320300059X">Robust  Monetary Policy with Competing Reference Models</a>,&rdquo; <em>Journal of Monetary  Economics</em>, July, 50: 945–75.</p>
<p><a name="footnote10"></a><sup>10</sup> IEEE  Computer Society, 1991. <a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/182763">IEEE  Standard Computer Dictionary: A Compilation of IEEE Standard Computer  Glossaries</a>, in <em>IEEE Std. 610</em>, 1-217.</p>
<p><a name="footnote11"></a><sup>11</sup> See,  for example (and references therein), Levin, Andrew T., Wieland, V., and  Williams, J. C., 1999. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.nber.org/system/files/chapters/c7418/c7418.pdf">Robustness of  Simple Monetary Policy Rules under Model Uncertainty</a>,&rdquo; in <em>Monetary  Policy Rules</em>, ed. John B. Taylor, University of Chicago Press, 1–14,  263–99; Levin, Andrew T., Wieland, V., and Williams, J. C., 2003. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/000282803322157016">The  Performance of Forecast-Based Monetary Policy Rules Under Model Uncertainty</a>,&rdquo; <em>American Economic Review,</em> June, 93(3): 622-45; Levin, Andrew T.,  Onatski, A., Williams, J. C., and Williams, N., 2006. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/3585423?seq=1">Monetary Policy Under  Uncertainty in Micro-Founded Macroeconomic Models</a>,&rdquo; <em>NBER Macroeconomics  Annual 2005</em>, ed., Mark Gertler and Kenneth Rogoff, University of Chicago  Press, 229-87; Taylor, John B. and Williams, J. C., 2011. &ldquo;<a href="https://web.stanford.edu/~johntayl/Onlinepaperscombinedbyyear/2011/Simple_and_Robust_Rules_for_Monetary_Policy.pdf">Simple  and Robust Rules for Monetary Policy</a>,&rdquo; in <em>Handbook of Monetary  Economics, Volume 3B</em>, eds. Benjamin Friedman and Michael Woodford, 829-60.</p>
<p><a name="footnote12"></a><sup>12</sup> Orphanides,  Athanasios, and Williams, J. C., 2004. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.nber.org/system/files/chapters/c9559/c9559.pdf">Imperfect  Knowledge, Inflation Expectations, and Monetary Policy</a>,&rdquo; in <em>The  Inflation-Targeting Debate</em>, eds. Ben S. Bernanke and Michael Woodford,  University of Chicago Press, 201-34; Orphanides, Athanasios and Williams, J.  C., 2005. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1094202505000086">Inflation  Scares and Forecast-Based Monetary Policy</a>,&rdquo; <em>Review of Economic Dynamics</em>,  April, 8(2): 498-527.</p>
<p><a name="footnote13"></a><sup>13</sup> &ldquo;Orphanides,  Athanasios and Williams, J. C., 2005. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0165188905001107">The  Decline of Activist Stabilization Policy: Natural Rate Misperceptions,  Learning, and Expectations</a>,&rdquo; <em>Journal of Economic Dynamics and Control</em>,  November, 29(11): 1927-50.</p>
<p><a name="footnote14"></a><sup>14</sup> With  a lower degree of uncertainty, a hybrid approach that combines elements of the  standard Taylor Rule and a difference rule performs well. See Orphanides,  Athanasios and Williams, J. C., 2002. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.brookings.edu/wp-content/uploads/2002/06/2002b_bpea_orphanides.pdf">Robust  Monetary Policy Rules with Unknown Natural Rates</a>,&rdquo; <em>Brookings Papers on  Economic Activity</em>, 2: 63-145; Orphanides, Athanasios and Williams, J. C.,  2006. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/40005102?seq=1">Monetary Policy  with Imperfect Knowledge</a>,&rdquo; <em>Journal of the European Economic Association</em>,  4(2/3): 366–75. </p>
<p><a name="footnote15"></a><sup>15</sup> Orphanides,  Athanasios and Williams, J. C., 2007. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304393207000566">Robust  Monetary Policy with Imperfect Knowledge</a>,&rdquo; <em>Journal of Monetary Economics</em>,  July, 54(5): 1406-35; Orphanides, Athanasios and Williams, J. C., 2013. &ldquo;<a href="https://www.nber.org/system/files/chapters/c9176/c9176.pdf">Monetary  Policy Mistakes and the Evolution of Inflation Expectations</a>,&rdquo; in <em>The  Great Inflation: The Rebirth of Modern Central Banking</em>, eds. Michael D.  Bordo and Athanasios Orphanides, University of Chicago Press, 255-97.</p>    </div>
         

